//! Entries slice -- Batch 149 (Plan 135 / Research 175) — the `sm_89` spill-law
//! / nodecode-probe STANDALONE pack: the three measured LAWS of the riir-ai
//! Plan-597 close (Benches 895 + 933) as independent rules. Sibling-batch
//! note: Batch 148 (the Bakhshaliyev series, Plan 133 / Research 174) landed
//! the same window from the same intake lead and embedded these laws'
//! EVIDENCE as the route-split regime content of `occupancy-before-async-
//! consumer-ada` -- this batch ships the laws THEMSELVES as standalone
//! decision rules (the dual-allocation was yielded per the Issue-665
//! discipline; the non-duplicate delta is this file + one rider; the yield
//! record: Research 175). The standalone form serves the retrievals the
//! integrator rule cannot answer: pricing a FUSION's register need (B895's
//! original context -- not an occupancy buy), running a sub-path ceiling
//! probe (methodology, any campaign), and pricing ANY tile-size decision
//! (bandwidth/latency regimes included). Corpus 616 -> 619 (E 55 -> 57,
//! D 134 -> 135).
//!
//! Quote authority: riir-ai `.benchmarks/895_*` + `.benchmarks/933_*`
//! (read-only citations; the owning lanes keep their code -- kernel-level
//! probes stay in riir-gpu with no production dispatch, v10t remains the
//! default). Benchability: the measured tables ARE the decision content --
//! no re-bench in this repo (`kernel_verify` is a STUB by design). Intra-doc
//! variance carried honestly: B933 §4's table cell reads 0.516x while §7's
//! recap rounds the same arm 0.522x across probe re-runs -- the LAW is the
//! four measurements; individual cells vary ~1% run-to-run.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::corpus::KernelRule;

pub fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ============================================================
        // -- spill-fragility-law-accumulator-regcap-cliff -- Batch 149 /
        // Research 175 / Plan 135 (B895 + B933 x4).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "spill-fragility-law-accumulator-regcap-cliff".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "The register file prices every per-thread budget squeeze on a tiled mma body, and the price is a CLIFF, not a slope: forcing the per-thread register count below the working set (via __launch_bounds__(threads, min_blocks) for occupancy, or any added register need at a pinned geometry) makes the compiler spill the accumulator set to local memory, and the spill bytes sit in the INNERMOST k-group loop -- every spilled accumulator touch becomes a local-memory round-trip per iteration, so tens of bytes cost HALF the kernel. Measured four times on the sm_89 q8 ternary-mma family (4090): 104 B -> 0.550x (v11gs, B895), 192 B -> 0.328x / 208 B -> 0.335x (v11gu/v11gut, B895 -- the +32-reg FUSION need, not an occupancy buy), 56 B -> 0.516x (v10o3, __launch_bounds__(256, 3)'s 85-reg cap on a 64-reg-accumulator body, B933 §4), 104 B -> 0.340x (the nodecode twin, B933 §6 -- without the decode dependency chain the scheduler stretches differently and spills MORE: 104 vs 56 B on the identical tile). The envelope arithmetic predicts it pre-compile: accumulators alone are 64 regs/thread for a 32x32 f16-out warp tile (dh[2][4][4] i32 + o[2][4][4] f32), the decode/fragment working set adds ~64 more, and 512 threads x 128 regs = 65,536 = the ENTIRE sm_89 file -- so any 512-thread geometry pinned at 128 regs has ZERO slack, and min_blocks >= 2 at 256 threads caps at 85 regs which a 64-acc tile cannot fit. Two escape hatches, both measured: (1) LOWER minBlocks to raise the ceiling -- __launch_bounds__(256, 1) allows 255 regs/thread and landed a 190-reg spill-free body (v11gq, B895 -- the fusion was itself refuted at parity, but SPILL-FREE at 8 warps/SM: occupancy 16 -> 8 warps cost NOTHING, B895's T2 -- 'Warps are not the wall; issue slots are'); (2) shrink the accumulator tile to fit under the cap -- which the tile economics then price separately (see tile-shrink-occupancy-pays-feed-economics, Batch 149: the spill-free 24-warp 32-acc body still lost 35%). The probe discipline: after compiling any launch-bounds variant, read num_regs() AND local_size_bytes() -- local_size > 0 rejects the variant BEFORE benchmarking (a spilled body that wins a noisy cell is a body you mispriced); all four cliff cells above were predictable from the envelope arithmetic alone. Regime: any register-dense mma body (accumulator sets sized by the warp tile) on silicon where the register file caps blocks/SM; register-LIGHT bodies sit outside the law's reach -- the law binds exactly when accumulators + working set approach the per-thread budget. Distinct from occupancy-before-async-consumer-ada (Batch 148 -- the ROUTE-SPLIT INTEGRATOR for occupancy purchases: smem route wins, register route spills, tile route loses on feed; it cites this law as its register-route evidence -- THIS rule is the law itself, applicable wherever a cap or added register need meets a full accumulator set, B895's fusion context included, occupancy irrelevant), from m-group-shared-weight-scan-register-relief (B110 -- the FIX relieving a register-cliff skinny GEMM by splitting M across thread groups; this is the LAW pricing any cap that forces spill, applied BEFORE choosing a fix), from occupancy-grid-cap-refuted-uncapped-default (B58 -- capping the GRID for throughput; this caps the REGISTER FILE per thread), from register-budget-head-split-gqa-reread-trade (B100 -- a head-split trade to fit a merge surface; here the tile's own accumulator set is the overflow), from independent-accumulator-chains-hide-load-latency-at-capped-occupancy (B108 -- latency-hiding AT a supply cap; this prices the attempt to RAISE the cap), from fold-query-tokens-for-occupancy (B23 -- folding work to fill idle SMs; no register cap involved), and from grid-collapsing-mega-kernel-occupancy-trap (B80 -- serializing away the grid). Source: riir-ai .benchmarks/895_issue902_ffn_fused_gate_up_refuted.md (the ladder table :20-26, the 512x128 structurally-dead note :28-31, 'Any accumulator-set overflow is catastrophic (the spill sits in the innermost group loop)' :47-50, T2 occupancy-not-the-limiter :42-46) + .benchmarks/933_plan597_s0_rung_a_occupancy.md (§3 envelope :61-77, §4 rung-A cells :79-104, §6 the spilled nodecode twin :132-152, §7 the four-times recap :165-231). Batch 149 / Plan 135 / Research 175.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: min_blocks=3 buys a third resident block (24 warps/SM) on\n// paper -- but the cap it imposes (65536 / (256 * 3) = 85 regs/thread)\n// is below the body's working set, so the compiler spills 56 B of the\n// accumulator set into the innermost k-group loop and throughput halves\n// (B933 §4: v10o3 0.516x vs v10t -- the spill-fragility cliff).\nfn tune_launch_naive() -> LaunchBounds {\n    LaunchBounds { threads: 256, min_blocks: 3 } // 85-reg cap vs ~128-reg body\n}\n\nstruct LaunchBounds {\n    threads: usize,\n    min_blocks: usize,\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: price the cap BEFORE benchmarking. The accumulator set\n// alone is 64 regs/thread at a 32x32 warp tile (dh[2][4][4] + o[2][4][4]);\n// the working set adds ~64. If per_thread_cap < needed, the variant WILL\n// spill -- reject it on local_size_bytes() > 0 after compile, or lower\n// min_blocks (the __launch_bounds__(256, 1) 255-reg escape landed 190 regs\n// spill-free, B895 v11gq; 8 warps/SM matched 16 -- warps were not the wall).\nfn tune_launch(threads: usize, wanted_blocks: usize, regs_needed: usize) -> LaunchBounds {\n    let reg_file: usize = 65_536; // sm_89: 512 x 128 = the entire file\n    let cap = reg_file / (threads * wanted_blocks);\n    if cap >= regs_needed {\n        LaunchBounds { threads, min_blocks: wanted_blocks } // spill-free\n    } else {\n        // keep the geometry that fits: fewer blocks, full registers\n        let blocks = (reg_file / (threads * regs_needed)).max(1);\n        LaunchBounds { threads, min_blocks: blocks }\n    }\n}\n\nfn reject_if_spilled(local_size_bytes: usize) -> bool {\n    local_size_bytes > 0 // a spilled variant is rejected before any timing\n}\n\nstruct LaunchBounds {\n    threads: usize,\n    min_blocks: usize,\n}\n".to_string(),
        },

        // ============================================================
        // -- nodecode-arm-bounds-overhead-elimination -- Batch 149 /
        // Research 175 / Plan 135 (B933 §6 + B895 addendum).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "nodecode-arm-bounds-overhead-elimination".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "Before building any 'take sub-path X off the dependency chain' arm -- warp specialization, producer/consumer splits, precompute-off-loop, a cheaper decode -- MEASURE X's ceiling first with a stripped variant: ship the body with X replaced by raw passthrough (VALUES GARBAGE BY DESIGN, never dispatched by any production path, gated out of every default route), run it in the SAME interleaved median-of-N harness as the full body, and read the delta. That delta is the FULL-ELIMINATION ceiling: no restructure that keeps X functioning can beat removing X entirely, so every dependency-chain arm on X is bounded by it. Measured on the sm_89 q8 ternary-mma prefill kernel (4090): decode share 7.3% at p=2048 (v10t 0.9225 ms vs nodecode 0.855 ms) and 8.4% at p=4096 (1.823 vs 1.670), B933 §6; the earlier same-probe cells measured the elimination-SPEEDUP form at +10.7% / +17.6% (B895 addendum: 0.9385 vs 0.848, 1.9685 vs 1.674) -- same probe, different sessions, the ORDER is what survives (single-digit share of wall at production shapes). The decisive use: the nodecode ceiling is 427 TF = 64.6% of the 660.6 int8 dense peak -- even ZERO decode sits at the bottom of the external 65-80% same-silicon band -- so the residual wall is feed ILP at 4 warps/scheduler, and the entire warp-spec producer/consumer arm is bounded by the 7.3% decode subset it could take off the consumer chain (below a +10% gate on its own; B933 §5's bounded disposition). THREE measurement traps, all lived: (1) the pair harness prints PAIR/single ratios -- the honest math is SINGLE-vs-SINGLE (B933 §6's explicit correction); (2) the stripped body RE-SCHEDULES -- the nodecode twin of the spilled rung-A body spilled MORE (104 vs 56 B: 'without the decode dependency chain the scheduler stretches differently'), so a stripped variant's register/spill state is its own datum, never interchangeable with the full body's; (3) a stripped variant that dispatches garbage is a production bug -- gate it out of every route (the in-repo nodecode arms live under test-only launchers). Regime: any recurring optimization campaign against a fixed kernel family where multiple arms attack the same sub-path; the probe costs one build + one harness run and retires (or prices) a whole arm class. Distinct from occupancy-before-async-consumer-ada (Batch 148 -- the occupancy route-split decision; it CONSUMES this probe's 64.6% ceiling + 7.3% share as regime evidence -- THIS rule is the probe METHODOLOGY itself, applicable to any sub-path in any campaign), from work-scaling-probe-launch-bound (B82 -- the token-count scaling probe deciding launch-bound vs arithmetic-bound ROUTING; this measures ONE sub-path's share at fixed shape), from profile-sol-table-before-compute-rung (B144 -- profiler SOL routing to the top limiter CLASS; this is the surgical ceiling for one NAMED sub-path, composable after the SOL table names it), and from tile-loop-back-edge-needs-multi-tile-bitexact-gate (B57 -- a fixture-population correctness gate; this is a perf-measurement instrument). Source: riir-ai .benchmarks/933_plan597_s0_rung_a_occupancy.md (§6 :132-163 the share math + the single-vs-single correction + the 64.6% ceiling + the spilled-twin re-scheduling datum; §5 :106-131 the bounded warp-spec disposition) + .benchmarks/895_issue902_ffn_fused_gate_up_refuted.md (addendum :80-100 the +10.7/+17.6% cells, VALUES GARBAGE BY DESIGN :83-85). Batch 149 / Plan 135 / Research 175.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: propose a warp-specialization restructure (producer decodes\n// off the consumer chain) from intuition -- \"decode is probably the\n// wall\" -- without ever measuring what removing decode entirely buys.\nfn plan_arm_naive(arms: &mut Vec<Arm>) {\n    arms.push(Arm {\n        name: \"warp-spec producer/consumer\",\n        expected_gain: 0.25, // guessed, never measured\n    });\n}\n\nstruct Arm {\n    name: &'static str,\n    expected_gain: f64,\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: the nodecode probe first. Ship the stripped variant\n// (passthrough decode, VALUES GARBAGE BY DESIGN, gated out of production\n// dispatch), run it single-vs-single in the same interleaved harness, and\n// read the share: share = (t_full - t_stripped) / t_full. Every\n// chain-removal arm on that sub-path is bounded by the share -- warp-spec\n// cannot beat REMOVING decode entirely (measured 7.3-8.4%, B933 §6; the\n// nodecode ceiling itself sits at 64.6% of the 660.6 int8 dense peak).\nfn plan_arm_probed(t_full_ms: f64, t_nodecode_ms: f64, gate: f64) -> Option<Arm> {\n    let share = (t_full_ms - t_nodecode_ms) / t_full_ms; // single-vs-single\n    // the full-elimination ceiling bounds every partial-removal arm\n    if share >= gate {\n        Some(Arm {\n            name: \"warp-spec producer/consumer\",\n            expected_gain: share, // bounded by measurement, never guessed\n        })\n    } else {\n        None // bounded below the gate -- retire the arm class, record why\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Arm {\n    name: &'static str,\n    expected_gain: f64,\n}\n".to_string(),
        },

        // ============================================================
        // -- tile-shrink-occupancy-pays-feed-economics -- Batch 149 /
        // Research 175 / Plan 135 (B933 §7).
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "tile-shrink-occupancy-pays-feed-economics".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "Shrinking the accumulator tile to buy occupancy (fit the reg cap, spill nothing, add warps) is priced by FEED ECONOMICS, and the price can exceed the buy: halving the warp tile (32x32 -> 32x16, nf 4 -> 2) halves per-thread accumulators 64 -> 32 regs and admits min_blocks=3 spill-free -- but every mma's fragment feed now covers half the useful area, so ldmatrix + A-LDS traffic PER USEFUL MMA doubles (the general law: fragment feed scales as 1 / tile-area-per-warp), and the halved row-block doubles the x-grid so each weight slab is staged by 2x the y-blocks (B staging doubles). Measured on the 4090 sm_89 q8 family (B933 §7): the v12 body -- 64-row blocks x 16-tok warps, 32 accumulator regs (dh[2][2][4] + o[2][2][4]), TOKS=64/NWARP=8/MINB=3 = 24 warps/SM, 75 regs, 0 local bytes, 25,088 B smem x 3 blocks -- is the FIRST spill-free 24-warp body in the family, and it STILL lost 35% (1.614 ms / 226.2 TF / 34.2% vs the 16-warp v10t 1.046 ms / 348.9 TF / 52.8% = 0.648x). The nodecode twin ran 0.656x -- stripping ALL decode recovers ~1% at that geometry, proving the wall is the tile economics, not decode: 'the extra 8 warps/SM cannot pay for their own feed.' Together with the spill law (spill-fragility-law-accumulator-regcap-cliff, Batch 149) this closes the structure in BOTH directions: raising warps at fixed tile spills (the cliff), removing the spill by shrinking the tile doubles the feed -- the 16-warp/128-reg geometry is a LOCAL OPTIMUM bounded by the register file on both sides, and the honest close is to measure both rejection cells and record the local optimum rather than keep buying occupancy. The bounded reopen conditions are part of the rule: a <=32-reg-acc body clearing +10% at its measured cell would reopen it (v12 measured the contrary on the one register-file route), and sm_90+ silicon reopens the whole envelope (TMA + setmaxnreg change both the register arithmetic and the staging economics -- the Machete class becomes portable). Regime: tiled mma bodies whose occupancy is register-capped, where fragment-feed traffic scales with 1/(tile area per warp); memory-bound bodies or register-light bodies whose feed does not scale that way sit outside. Distinct from occupancy-before-async-consumer-ada (Batch 148 -- the occupancy ROUTE-SPLIT integrator; it carries this rule's v12 cell as its tile-route refutation evidence -- THIS rule is the tile-economics LAW itself, pricing tile-size decisions that are NOT occupancy buys too: bandwidth/latency tile choices, smaller-tile twins, remainder routing), from spill-fragility-law-accumulator-regcap-cliff (Batch 149 companion -- the law prices caps that SPILL; this prices the shrink that AVOIDS the spill and loses anyway), from batch-width-routed-dual-tile-twins (B77 -- ROUTING between two tile shapes by workload width; this is the trade that decides whether the smaller twin is worth having), from expert-remainder-routed-to-smaller-tile-twins (remainder routing; a dispatch concern, not a tile-size decision), from pad-tail-to-fixed-tile (fixed-tail correctness, not occupancy), from stream-k-persistent-cta-mac-segments (B102 -- work decomposition across CTAs; this is per-warp tile AREA), and from row-tile-amortize-input-load (amortizing loads over a BIGGER row tile -- the measured counter-direction of the same axis). Source: riir-ai .benchmarks/933_plan597_s0_rung_a_occupancy.md (§7 :165-203 the v12 cells + the feed mechanism + the both-directions local-optimum close; :205-231 the S4.b honesty split + the two reopen triggers). Batch 149 / Plan 135 / Research 175.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: halve the warp tile so min_blocks=3 fits (32 accumulator\n// regs, spill-free 24 warps/SM) and assume the extra warps win. They do\n// not: the halved tile doubles ldmatrix + A-LDS per useful mma and doubles\n// B staging across the doubled x-grid (B933 §7: 0.648x -- spill-free and\n// still down 35%).\nfn pick_tile_naive() -> Tile {\n    Tile { rows: 32, cols: 16, acc_regs: 32 } // halved to fit the 85-reg cap\n}\n\nstruct Tile {\n    rows: usize,\n    cols: usize,\n    acc_regs: usize,\n}\n".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// optimized: price the feed before shipping the shrink. Fragment loads\n// per useful mma scale as 1 / (tile area per warp): halving the tile\n// doubles ldmatrix + A-LDS work per mma, and halving the row-block doubles\n// the grid axis that re-stages each weight slab. Accept the smaller tile\n// only when occupancy_gain > feed_penalty -- measured on sm_89 it was not\n// (0.648x, B933 §7): the 16-warp geometry is the local optimum.\nfn pick_tile(big: Tile, small: Tile, occupancy_gain: f64, feed_penalty: f64) -> Tile {\n    if occupancy_gain > feed_penalty {\n        small\n    } else {\n        big // 24 vs 16 warps cannot pay for their own feed here\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Tile {\n    rows: usize,\n    cols: usize,\n    acc_regs: usize,\n}\n".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
